WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.313
Capacity planning should begin with measurements from each backend.

00:00:03.313 --> 00:00:07.598
Useful signals include prompt processing speed, generation speed, time to first token,

00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:12.239
total response time, active requests, queue length, CPU use, GPU use, VRAM pressure,

00:00:12.239 --> 00:00:14.024
memory pressure, and failure rate.

00:00:14.024 --> 00:00:19.023
Tokens per second is useful, but it does not describe the whole user experience.

00:00:19.023 --> 00:00:20.451
Context length changes performance.

00:00:20.451 --> 00:00:24.735
A long document prompt can consume far more memory and processing time

00:00:24.735 --> 00:00:26.520
than a short chat message.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.805
A service may benchmark well with small prompts and then struggle with

00:00:30.805 --> 00:00:32.947
real document analysis or coding-agent sessions.

00:00:32.947 --> 00:00:38.302
Testing should include the actual types of work the load-balanced endpoint is expected to serve.

00:00:38.302 --> 00:00:41.159
Concurrency limits protect the service from slow collapse.

00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:45.443
Every backend reaches a point where adding more simultaneous requests makes everyone

00:00:45.443 --> 00:00:47.228
wait longer or causes failures.

00:00:47.228 --> 00:00:51.869
Runtime batching and parallelism can help in some environments, but they need measurement.

00:00:51.869 --> 00:00:56.154
More concurrency is not automatically better if it increases time to first

00:00:56.154 --> 00:00:57.939
token or causes out-of-memory behavior.

00:00:57.939 --> 00:00:59.724
Warm and cold states matter.

00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:05.437
A model already loaded in memory can respond much faster than one being read from disk.

00:01:05.437 --> 00:01:09.007
A freshly restarted server may be online but not ready.

00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:12.577
Capacity planning should combine benchmark data with representative workloads: chat,

00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:15.790
coding assistants, batch jobs, long contexts, and mixed traffic.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.718
The goal is to know when to route, queue, shed load,

00:01:19.718 --> 00:01:21.860
or ask clients to retry later.
